module bindings.python.sso_setup_attach_session_pin;

// Sentry's AuthIdentityHandler attaches a provider identity to a local
// account, and its `user` property names the account the identity lands on
// by resolving the provider-asserted email (`resolve_email_to_user`): the
// address the upstream account currently carries picks the local account it
// resolves to, which is the exposure OpenID Connect Core 5.7 describes for
// the `email` claim. `handle_attach_identity` is the attach entry point and
// states its own contract -- "Given an already authenticated user, attach or
// re-attach an identity" -- so the attaching flow is the one that must
// decide who that already-authenticated user is.
//
// The login flow honours the contract: it attaches only under
// `self.request.user.id == self.user.id`, requiring the provider-resolved
// account to match the session user. The SSO setup flow passed the provider
// identity through unmodified (`self.auth_handler(identity)
// .handle_attach_identity(om)`), so during provider configuration -- where
// an authenticated admin is already acting -- the address the IdP asserted
// about anyone chose the account that received the identity, and an
// identity set up on a victim's address linked straight to the victim. The
// fix pins the resolution to the session (`{**identity, "email":
// request.user.email}`), the email-shaped spelling of the same match the
// login flow checks by id.
//
// The binding requires one of the two session-match controls the API's own
// callers use: the session-email pin on Django's documented `request.user`
// chain, or a resolved-user id equality in either operand order. Both are
// the library's spellings, not the remediation's: the pin is keyed on the
// attribute chain, so it survives any renaming of the local it is assigned
// to, and the equality matches with or without the `self.` prefix.
//
// Residual, stated rather than hidden, false-negative direction only: a
// pin read through an intermediate local (`u = request.user; ... u.email`)
// is not seen, and the equality is matched as text over the comparison, so
// an equivalent guard phrased as a `!=` early return is missed. A
// docstring or comment cannot satisfy either negation -- both clauses read
// AST-derived facts, which prose produces none of.
binding ssoSetupAttachUnpinnedSessionEmail {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "python"
    and node.context.call == "handle_attach_identity"
    and not (node.context.selector contains "request.user.email")
    and not (containsAny(node.context.binary, [
      "request.user.id==self.user.id",
      "self.user.id==request.user.id",
      "request.user.id==user.id",
      "user.id==request.user.id",
    ]))
  emit issue code.UnverifiedEmailClaimIdentity at node
}
